  "titulo": "The lighthouse keeper",
  "ppm": 130,
  "texto": "# The island\n@energy 2\n> Narrate calmly, like telling a story by the fire.\n\nElvira had looked after the lighthouse on Crow Island for thirty years.\nShe lived alone, with a gray cat, an old radio and a library of worn-out books.\nEvery evening she climbed the hundred and twelve steps of the tower to switch on the light.\nShe had only one rule: the lighthouse light never goes out.\n[soft]She used to say the sea spoke to her, and that you had to know how to listen.[/soft]\nThat autumn evening, the sky turned a **strange** color, somewhere between green and purple.\nThe gulls flew low, and the wind carried the smell of rain.\nElvira closed the windows and checked the generator twice.\n\"It's going to be a long night,\" she told the cat.\nThe cat didn't answer, but it hid under the bed.\nDown below, the waves began to pound the rocks and the beach with real **force**.\n\n# The storm\n@energy 3\n> The tension builds.\n\nAt midnight, the storm hit the island with all its fury.\nThe rain beat against the windows as if someone were trying to get in.\nAll at once, the radio crackled and a desperate voice filled the kitchen.\n\"This is the fishing boat Southern Star. We can't see a thing, we're near the rocks!\"\nElvira ran up the tower.\n[pause 2s]\nThe lighthouse was **dark**.\nLightning had damaged the generator, and the great lamp wouldn't respond.\nOut among the waves, a tiny red light was drifting toward the rocks.\nThat boat was heading straight for the **reef**.\nHow could she guide them without a light? [up]\n\n# The light\n@energy 4\n> The climax: firmness and urgency.\n\nThen she remembered the oil lamp her grandfather had kept in the cellar.\nShe went down to find it, wiped it with her sleeve and lit it with trembling hands.\nShe climbed the hundred and twelve steps again, soaked and out of breath.\nShe set the lamp in front of the great mirror and began to turn it by hand.\nIt was heavy, but it moved.\nA **faint** beam of light swept out to sea, then another, and another.\n[pause 2s]\nThe tiny red light hesitated for a moment, then changed course.\n\"I see you! You're turning!\" Elvira shouted, though no one could hear her.\nShe kept turning the mirror all night long, until her arms ached.\nAt dawn, the fishing boat sailed into the harbor on the other side of the island, **safe** and sound.\n\n# The visit\n@energy 2\n> Calm after the storm. End tenderly.\n\nTwo days later, a small boat pulled up to the island's dock.\nIt was Simon, the captain of the fishing boat, with his daughter and a basket of bread, cheese and fruit.\n\"You saved our lives,\" he said, taking off his cap.\nElvira smiled and showed him the old oil lamp, her new treasure.\n\"To be honest, it was my grandfather who saved you.\"\nIn the afternoon they drank tea in the kitchen while the cat slept by the **stove**.\nThe captain's daughter asked if she could climb the tower.\n[soft]From the top, the sea looked calm, as if it had never been angry at all.[/soft]\nBefore leaving, Simon promised to come back every autumn.\nAnd he kept his word: every year, when the sky turns strange, a boat arrives with **fresh** bread.\nElvira waits for it on the dock, with the oil lamp still **burning**. [down]\n"
