  "texto": "# The discovery\n@energy 2\n> Narrate calmly.\n\nJulian had been working as the town's mail carrier for only a month.\nThat week, the post office was being renovated, and the old furniture had to be moved.\nWhen they pushed a heavy cabinet aside, an envelope covered in dust fell to the floor.\nIt had an old stamp and a date from **forty** years ago.\nIt was addressed to Carmen Ibanez, fourteen Orange Tree Street.\n\"It must have been stuck behind the cabinet,\" said the head of the office.\nJulian looked at the envelope for a long time.\nNo one had opened it, and the handwriting was **careful**, almost shy.\nCould that woman still be living there? [up]\nThat afternoon, after finishing his route, he decided to find out.\n\n# The search\n@energy 3\n> Curiosity grows.\n\nAt number fourteen on Orange Tree Street, there was now a bakery.\nThe owner didn't know any Carmen, but she called her mother on the phone.\n\"Of course! Carmen lives in the blue house next to the church,\" came the answer.\nJulian crossed the square with the envelope in his hand and his heart racing.\nPeople in the neighborhood waved at him without knowing what he was carrying.\nThe door was opened by a woman with white hair, wearing an apron covered in flour.\n[pause 2s]\n\"Carmen Ibanez?\" Julian asked.\n\"That's me. Is something wrong?\"\nJulian explained the story and handed her the envelope.\nCarmen read the sender's **name** and stood very still.\nIt was Martin, the young man who had gone to work in the south when she was twenty.\nShe had never heard from **him** again.\n\n# The letter\n@energy 4\n> The climax: restrained emotion, no exaggeration.\n\nCarmen sat down in the kitchen and opened the envelope with care.\nShe read it aloud, her voice breaking.\n\"Carmen: the work in the south is hard, but I've saved enough for a small house.\"\n\"If you still love me, wait for me at the station on the first day of spring.\"\n[pause 2s]\nCarmen set the letter on the table.\n\"I thought he had forgotten me,\" she whispered. \"I waited a whole year.\"\n\"And he must have thought I didn't **want** to wait.\"\nJulian didn't know what to say.\nAt the bottom of the letter was an address in a city in the south.\n\"Maybe he still lives there!\" Julian said all of a sudden.\nCarmen looked at him with **shining** eyes.\n\"Then help me write to him!\"\n\n# The reply\n@energy 2\n> End tenderly.\n\nThat night they wrote a short letter together, with no frills.\nCarmen told him about her life: a happy marriage, two children, a garden and the calm of her seventies.\nAt the end, she added a single sentence: \"I got your letter. It arrived forty years late.\"\nJulian took it to the office himself the next day.\nThree weeks went by.\n[soft]Every morning, Carmen waited for the mail carrier at her door, with a cup of coffee for him.[/soft]\nOne Monday, Julian came running with an envelope in his hand.\nIt had the **same** careful handwriting, now a little shaky.\nCarmen didn't open it at the door.\nShe tucked it into her apron pocket like a treasure and smiled.\n\"I'll read this one alone,\" she said. \"But tomorrow I'll tell you **everything**.\" [down]\n"
